(1. Meaning and use) Tj
T*
(A negative prefix changes a word toward an opposite, negative, or incorrect meaning. English) Tj
T*
(uses several prefixes, so learning common patterns is more reliable than translating) Tj
T*
(directly from Spanish.) Tj
T*
(Un- is very common: happy ? unhappy; employed ? unemployed. In- often appears with Latin) Tj
T*
(roots: accurate ? inaccurate; dependent ? independent. Before m or p, in- commonly becomes) Tj
T*
(im-: possible ? impossible; patient ? impatient.) Tj
T*
(2. Bilingual examples) Tj
T*
(After losing his contract, he found himself unemployed for several months.) Tj
T*
(Después de perder su contrato, estuvo desempleado durante varios meses.) Tj
T*
(The information was inaccurate.) Tj
T*
(La información era imprecisa.) Tj
T*
(I misunderstood the question.) Tj
T*
(Malinterpreté la pregunta.) Tj
T*
(3. Rules and common mistakes) Tj
T*
(Dis- often expresses an opposite or absence: honest ? dishonest; agree ? disagree. Mis-) Tj
T*
(usually means wrongly or badly: understand ? misunderstand; spell ? misspell.) Tj
T*
(Do not invent a prefix by analogy. English says impossible, not "unpossible". Learn the) Tj
T*
(whole word when possible.) Tj
T*
(Study tip: Read the English sentence aloud first. Try to explain it before checking the) Tj
T*
(Spanish translation.) Tj
T*
