(1. Meaning and use) Tj
T*
(Nonetheless means nevertheless or even so. It introduces a statement that remains true) Tj
T*
(despite the information just mentioned.) Tj
T*
(At the beginning of a sentence it is normally followed by a comma. It can also follow a) Tj
T*
(semicolon when connecting two closely related independent clauses.) Tj
T*
(2. Bilingual examples) Tj
T*
(The task was difficult. Nonetheless, we completed it on time.) Tj
T*
(La tarea era difícil. No obstante, la terminamos a tiempo.) Tj
T*
(The schedule was demanding; nonetheless, everyone completed the course.) Tj
T*
(El horario era exigente; no obstante, todos completaron el curso.) Tj
T*
(The evidence was limited. Nonetheless, the team continued the study.) Tj
T*
(Las pruebas eran limitadas. No obstante, el equipo continuó el estudio.) Tj
T*
(3. Rules and common mistakes) Tj
T*
(Nonetheless is relatively formal. In everyday conversation, but, still, or even so may sound) Tj
T*
(more natural.) Tj
T*
(Do not add formal connectors merely to sound advanced. Use nonetheless when the contrast) Tj
T*
(between the two ideas is genuinely clear.) Tj
T*
(Study tip: Say the English example aloud before checking the Spanish translation.) Tj
T*
